00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.580
With the tariff risk now quantified,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.480
let's look at exactly how to protect a portfolio against it.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.600
Tariff risk hedging for Canadian exporters

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.800
involves screening holdings for US revenue exposure

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.860
above 15% and pairing that screen

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.800
with options based protection or sector rotation

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.580
into domestic focused names.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.900
Today's tariff threat on Ontario cosmetics

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.380
illustrates how sudden policy shifts

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.100
can widen bid-ask spreads

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.180
and trigger stop loss cascades

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.140
in small cap consumer stocks.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.160
The owner of the Ontario-based firm explicitly noted

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.860
that the newest US tariff threat is generating fresh fear

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.760
and confusion for small businesses

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.400
already managing elevated input costs

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.240
and broader economic pressure.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.900
Investors can implement the approach

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.080
by pulling US revenue percentages

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.160
from company filings inside a brokerage screener,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.100
then overlaying a 5% trailing stop

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.420
or buying protective puts on the most exposed names.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:00.720
The strategy performed best during the 2018-2019

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.000
trade war period when diversified Canadian portfolios

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.800
outperformed pure exporters by roughly 8 percentage points.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.780
Risks include over hedging in low volatility regimes

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.400
that erodes returns through premium decay,

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.620
always size the hedge to the actual percentage

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.180
of portfolio revenue at risk rather than blanket sector avoidance.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.420
Canadian investors holding TFSA positions in consumer exporters

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.740
should first quantify exact US shipment percentages

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.280
before deciding whether to reduce exposure

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.040
or add protective options.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.340
The mechanics of that hedge become even clearer

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.440
when we examine how tariff headlines

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.680
actually move small cap liquidity.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.340
Imagine you own shares

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.620
in an Ontario cosmetics exporter

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.300
that ships one-fifth of its output to the US.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.160
You place a market order the morning after the tariff headline.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.100
Your fill arrives 12 cents lower than the previous clothes

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.240
because the bid asks spread on that low float name

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.000
just widened from three cents to 18 cents

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.200
in the first 45 minutes of trading.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.320
The mechanism at work is reduced market maker inventory

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.180
when policy risks spikes.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.040
Dealers simply pull bids rather than absorb inventory

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.500
they cannot hedge quickly.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.240
Most retail investors do not realize

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.160
the spread expansion is largest in the first hour

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.520
and often reverses by midday once algorithmic liquidity returns.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.360
The professionals always check the average daily volume

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:33.920
versus the 20-day average

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:38.280
before entering any position larger than 1% of portfolio value.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.700
The biggest mistake with tariff driven volatility

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.680
is treating the initial price drop

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.120
as a permanent re-rating instead of a liquidity event.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.580
Instead, always wait for the spread to compress back

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.500
below its 10-day average before adding to the position.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.960
In practice, this means pulling up the level two

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.520
order book on your platform

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.280
and confirming that at least three market makers

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.880
have restored tight quotes before executing.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.300
The same dynamic appears in other low float Canadian names

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:10.800
whenever US policy headlines surface.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.780
So the discipline of waiting for spread normalization applies

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.980
across the entire small cap exporter universe.

